WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.394 --> 00:00:05.060
ADRIAN LUDWIG: My
name is Adrian Ludwig.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.600
I work at Google.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.170
I'm the director for
Android security.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.160
Let me be the 57th person
to welcome you to Next.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.440
Thank you all for
coming to what is

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.660
my hometown of San Francisco.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.330
I'm excited to
have you all here.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:23.690
We have 59 minutes
and 24 seconds

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.120
to chat a little bit
about Android security.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.090
So I'll probably spend
about 45 minutes or so

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.940
going through content,
and then hopefully we'll

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.850
have some time for
questions towards the end

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.560
to be able to go through.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.290
And would welcome any questions
that you might have, and make

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:40.789
sure that we get
you the information

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:43.730
that you need to be making good
decisions about what you're

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.900
doing in your environment.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.260
So I'm going to talk about
Android security, which

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.930
is a big topic.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.660
We throw around numbers
in the world of Android

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.740
that sometimes boggle my mind.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.550
You know, 1.4-plus billion
active users is an incredible

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.210
statistic.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:07.040
And that number grows
every day by amounts

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.549
that you can't really fathom.

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:11.090
The number of people
in San Francisco

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:13.160
doesn't begin to approach
the number of new users

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.250
that we add to Android as an
ecosystem on a daily basis.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.211
And so it's really
an honor to be

00:01:19.211 --> 00:01:20.960
able to talk a little
bit about what we've

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.690
been doing at Google, but also
talk a little bit about how

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.640
we fit into the overall
ecosystem in terms

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:26.840
of protecting Android users.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.274
Because we're just one
big but still very small

00:01:31.274 --> 00:01:33.440
part of how it is that we're
approaching protections

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.810
in the Android ecosystem.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.047
And so where do
we really fit in?

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:40.130
That's a good way to start
thinking about Android,

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.970
because it's very different
from other platforms.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.200
Google is a shepherd or
steward for an ecosystem.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.590
We're not the provider of the
platform in a strict sense.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:53.540
We're not the maker
of all of the devices

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.650
that get used by users.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.566
We're not the maker of
all of the applications

00:01:56.566 --> 00:01:58.400
that get used by users.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.672
And maybe this part's obvious,
we're not all of the users.

00:02:01.672 --> 00:02:03.380
And so there's a lot
of different parties

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:05.713
that are involved in thinking
about how to have security

00:02:05.713 --> 00:02:07.550
for an ecosystem of this scale.

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:11.120
We see our role as facilitating
application developers

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.070
by providing them
with a platform

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.040
that is safe by default,
that has APIs that allow them

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.662
to build very high
security applications,

00:02:18.662 --> 00:02:20.870
or even if they're not
thinking about security, to be

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.720
able to benefit from the
security of the platform.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:29.750
We see users as basically not
wanting to think about security

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.150
but having an expectation that
security will be provided.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.610
And so we've
designed the devices

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.560
to be secure by
default. And then

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.170
we've built layer upon layer
of protections in addition

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.960
to that that are really
transparent so that users

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:44.330
don't really need to
worry about security.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.430
And then on the
device maker side,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:49.280
we work very closely
with partners--

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.930
large companies,
small companies,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.080
individual developers,
to make sure

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.720
that the devices that get
out into the ecosystem

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.040
have security protections.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.080
And there's a variety
of tools that we have.

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.130
And we will release
to open source.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:05.310
But then we do things like build
the compatibility test suite,

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.980
and a compatibility
definition document

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.366
that can define the security
model across the Android

00:03:09.366 --> 00:03:11.240
ecosystem so that we
know there's consistency

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.759
across the ecosystem.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.300
And then one of the
things that we've

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.470
been investing a
lot in that I'll

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.690
spend a few minutes towards
the end talking about

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.990
is making sure that devices
are receiving security updates.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:25.370
And optimizing security updates
in an open source environment

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.580
turns out to be a pretty
interesting and complicated

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.010
problem.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.570
The security industry and
sort of the software industry

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.990
spent 10 or 15
years figuring out

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:40.062
how to deliver security updates
for closed source platforms.

00:03:40.062 --> 00:03:42.020
And Android has been, I
think, at the forefront

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.670
of figuring out how to do
that for open source as well.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.128
And so we'll talk
a bit about that.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.450
The way I tend to think
about the problem,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:54.510
because it's so big, and
because it's so diverse,

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.040
is to focus on three
different areas.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.360
The first is on the
platform itself, the code

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:03.629
that people think of as Android.

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:05.670
And so we'll spend some
time thinking about that.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:07.240
Then we'll get into
services, things

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.240
that are delivered as
applications, most of them

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.300
by Google, but also
by third parties that

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.010
add additional
security protections.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:16.079
And then the last
is what we've done

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:18.930
to work with other parties
in the ecosystem to make sure

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.486
that friction that can
sometimes impede security

00:04:21.486 --> 00:04:23.610
doesn't get in the way of
security being delivered,

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.860
and in particular,
around updates

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.570
and how we're enabling that.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:34.170
So my background, for
people who are curious,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:39.630
is in creation and
delivery of exploits,

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:42.130
going back quite a ways.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:45.390
And so I was sort of on the
more offensive side of security.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.592
And one of the things that
was really interesting

00:04:47.592 --> 00:04:49.050
from the offensive
side of security

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.590
is if you look at
security primitives,

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.564
basically 35-ish years
ago the US government

00:04:55.564 --> 00:04:57.480
put together something
called the Orange Book.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.520
And there are similar types of
things that were put together

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.603
by other governments--
that have been put together

00:05:01.603 --> 00:05:02.770
in other places as well.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:04.380
But there is a fairly
well-defined set

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.510
of expected security
primitives that

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.930
would exist on a secure device.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.020
And what's interesting is
it's taken a really long time

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.300
for the software industry
to evolve to a point

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.940
where it's actually providing
all those primitives.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.450
When I think about Android
and its sort of phase

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.700
in that evolution,
I put it right

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.770
next to what I think of as the
other mobile operating system.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.800
So Android, iOS, Chrome
OS, all uniformly

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.360
provide the same
primitives, which

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.000
are the ones that were described
almost 35 years ago in Orange

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.583
Book and these other
manuals, but that

00:05:37.583 --> 00:05:40.410
just took a really long time
for them to get broadly adopted.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.467
And those are isolation
of applications,

00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:45.300
making sure that there
was cryptographically

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.924
strong device integrity
that was rooted

00:05:46.924 --> 00:05:48.840
in a hardware route of trust.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.340
Then there was
something interesting

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.480
that happened in
those intervening

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.670
30 years, which was the
realization that there

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:55.119
were bugs in software.

00:05:55.119 --> 00:05:57.160
And so one of the areas
that we've invested very,

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.000
very heavily in is what we
refer to as exploit mitigation

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.820
and attack surface minimisation,
which is technology

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.742
is designed to make
sure that, even if we've

00:06:04.742 --> 00:06:06.450
made a mistake--
because let me tell you,

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:07.620
we make a lot of mistakes.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.280
Google is very good
at making mistakes.

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:10.821
That's why we have
launch and iterate

00:06:10.821 --> 00:06:11.949
as one of our philosophies.

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:13.740
That's why updates are
so important for all

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:16.570
these platforms-- but even
if there is a mistake,

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:19.440
hopefully exploit
mitigation can make

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.130
exploitation of that in the
real world more challenging.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.570
Over the last
couple of days we've

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.903
been looking very closely at
a number of vulnerabilities

00:06:26.903 --> 00:06:29.880
that we didn't know about
before that became public.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.550
And we've been sort of analyzing
those and thinking through--

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:34.781
how did exploit
mitigations prevent these

00:06:34.781 --> 00:06:37.030
from actually being able to
be used in the real world?

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.010
And what are the
technologies that we

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.426
need to be continuing to invest
in so that even if there's

00:06:40.426 --> 00:06:41.910
an issue that we
don't know about,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.210
we can prevent it from
being a successful exploit?

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.760
And that's an area that
we've invested in a lot.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.490
Management basically
comes down to enterprises

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:51.780
and applications.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.736
We want to know what the
state of a device is.

00:06:53.736 --> 00:06:55.110
And so we've been
investing a lot

00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:58.140
in making sure that those
APIs are available as well.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.317
When I look at Android--

00:06:59.317 --> 00:07:01.650
and the other mobile operating
systems for that matter--

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.340
I see, basically,
complete implementation

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.030
of all of the best practices
at a platform level.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.920
So we're actually in
a really good place

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.750
across the mobile
operating systems.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.880
That's very well and
very substantially

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:17.310
differentiated from either
server infrastructure

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:21.407
or from other
desktop environments.

00:07:21.407 --> 00:07:22.740
So we're in a pretty good place.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.984
One of the interesting
points, though,

00:07:24.984 --> 00:07:26.400
about the mobile
operating systems

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:29.130
is the velocity with which new
technology can be delivered.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:32.280
So I want to talk about one of
those new pieces of technology.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:36.910
Encryption is a feature
that has existed on Android

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:40.770
for, I think, about six years.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.590
It was on devices,
but it was not

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.824
enabled by default. Users
were able to turn it on.

00:07:45.824 --> 00:07:47.490
Enterprises demanded
that it be present.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.930
It was in the
checklist of things

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.388
that had to be on
a device in order

00:07:50.388 --> 00:07:52.020
to deploy it into an enterprise.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.930
But the reality was
nobody turned it on.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:59.580
That began to change roughly two
years ago with the Android 6.0

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.340
release where the default
configuration on Android

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:05.492
devices was to enable it for
devices that were capable of--

00:08:05.492 --> 00:08:07.200
from a performance
standpoint-- of having

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:09.074
it functioning and not
disruptive to the user

00:08:09.074 --> 00:08:09.990
experience.

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.270
And so in the span
of about two years

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.760
we went from basically
nobody having encryption

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.430
enabled on Android devices,
even though it was available,

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.520
to having over 80% of
new Android devices

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:22.470
that are coming
out be encrypted.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.294
And so that adoption
rate I think

00:08:24.294 --> 00:08:26.460
is one of the unique
characteristics from a security

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:28.260
standpoint of mobile--

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:31.740
is if we find some other
technology like encryption that

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.020
is really important
to defend users,

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:37.510
you can expect that it will
be adopted very, very quickly.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.620
And this is turnover rate,
not in a sort of slow--

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:42.630
or not in a highly
managed environment.

00:08:42.630 --> 00:08:44.430
This is for the
overall ecosystem.

00:08:44.430 --> 00:08:47.130
This is for hundreds
of millions of devices

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:49.900
and how quickly those security
features can be rolled out.

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.410
So having this sort of velocity
of security innovation,

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.910
I think, is one of
the things that's

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:59.280
really attractive about the
mobile platforms at this point.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.550
It's also one of the
reasons why investing

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:07.710
in innovative security research
is really important because--

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:11.970
this sounds trite, but we're
running out of areas to invest.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:12.780
We have encryption.

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:13.650
We have verified boot.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:14.608
We have good isolation.

00:09:14.608 --> 00:09:16.044
We have good sandboxing.

00:09:16.044 --> 00:09:17.460
We're basically
getting to a point

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:20.250
where we have hardware
route of trust,

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.020
where what is really important
is the flaws and the bugs.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.895
And so this is an
area that Google--

00:09:28.895 --> 00:09:30.087
and in particular Chrome--

00:09:30.087 --> 00:09:31.670
we're very, very
innovative about four

00:09:31.670 --> 00:09:33.086
or five years ago
in saying, we're

00:09:33.086 --> 00:09:35.150
going to deploy bug bounties.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.580
And Android at this point
is one of the largest bounty

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:39.120
programs in the world.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.120
One of the unique
characteristics of the Android

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.190
bounty program is the
amount of investment

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:45.380
we're seeing in
terms of researchers

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.660
in areas of the world that
historically have not been

00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:49.790
prominent in security research.

00:09:49.790 --> 00:09:52.536
I'm hinting at
China, specifically,

00:09:52.536 --> 00:09:55.160
where we're seeing a significant
number of the researchers that

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.840
are looking at Android and
finding vulnerabilities

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.470
on Android are based in China.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.920
So we've tapped into security
research and security

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:04.220
innovation that really
is not visible to any

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.410
of the other platform providers,
which is pretty interesting.

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:09.320
I have no doubt
that worldwide there

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:12.650
are people looking for the bugs,
but only on Android are they

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:14.600
actually then reporting
them into the vendor

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:18.090
and allowing us to make
the platform more secure.

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.840
So those are some of
the things that we're

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:21.790
doing at the platform level.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:23.680
I want to spend a
few minutes talking

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.800
as well about the
changes and sort of work

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.040
that we've done on services.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:32.140
And to provide a
little bit of context,

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.020
services historically
have been something

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.420
that is left to a third party.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.000
If you look at the
desktop platforms,

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:45.640
for example, Microsoft, Apple,
and the other desktop platform

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:50.260
providers said, let's create
an ecosystem of third party

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:50.980
vendors--

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.880
Symantec, McAfee, Kaspersky,
et cetera, et cetera, right?

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.820
Dozens, if not hundreds,
of those security research

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:01.430
and anti-virus and
security providers.

00:11:01.430 --> 00:11:04.930
And that was a model that
worked relatively well.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:08.320
But it had significant gaps
in terms of the coverage

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.322
that those solutions
were able to provide,

00:11:10.322 --> 00:11:12.280
and in terms of the
ability for those solutions

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.850
to influence the
direction of the platform

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.670
and also respond to knowledge
that the platform provider had.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:20.800
So about five to
six years ago when

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.620
we were looking at Android,
we said, you know what?

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.800
Rather than having that
capability be entirely

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:30.660
external to the platform
provided by third parties,

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.580
let's make sure that we provide
a best of breed set of security

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:36.550
services integrated
with the applications

00:11:36.550 --> 00:11:38.300
that we're putting
onto these devices.

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.750
And so Google Play and
Google Play Services

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:43.494
incorporated a suite
of security services

00:11:43.494 --> 00:11:45.910
and made it available for all
devices that have the Google

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:48.250
applications on them.

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.900
That's a very different
model than what

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.167
we've seen on other platforms
in the mobile space, where

00:11:53.167 --> 00:11:55.750
instead, those other platforms
have said security services are

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:59.260
basically forboden, or are
very significantly impeded.

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.375
They're not provided by
the platform provider.

00:12:01.375 --> 00:12:03.250
And in fact, it's very
difficult to implement

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:04.291
on those other platforms.

00:12:04.291 --> 00:12:07.090
And so Android has taken a
very different direction there.

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.006
And it's one that, I
think, over time is going

00:12:09.006 --> 00:12:10.550
to be really, really important.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:12.204
So when I talk about
security services,

00:12:12.204 --> 00:12:13.870
and I've given you
sort of that framing,

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:15.610
here are the types of
services that we're

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.310
providing right now, out
of the box, on any device

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.410
that has Google Play on it.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.290
The first one is what
we call Verify Apps.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.150
Security is not an
area where we've had

00:12:25.150 --> 00:12:26.965
a ton of marketing investments.

00:12:26.965 --> 00:12:29.380
So these are not
super sexy names.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.394
We need to add some
X's and Y's and things

00:12:31.394 --> 00:12:32.560
to make these more exciting.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.570
Verify Apps does exactly
what you would expect.

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:36.028
It verifies the
apps that are being

00:12:36.028 --> 00:12:37.660
installed onto the device.

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:40.810
In addition to checking
the certificate that's

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.080
used to sign the
application, Verify Apps

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.380
checks with Google at
the time of installation

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.910
to see whether this is an
application that exposes

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:51.160
the user to a heightened risk.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:56.140
Is it-- I'll use the sort of
desktop nomenclature-- is it

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:56.764
a virus?

00:12:56.764 --> 00:12:58.180
Turns out on Android
we don't have

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.876
viruses for various reasons,
inclusive of the fact

00:13:00.876 --> 00:13:01.750
that apps are signed.

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:03.850
And so the viral
mutation that is

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.290
the heart of why we call
things viruses doesn't apply.

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:07.811
But is it a piece of malware?

00:13:07.811 --> 00:13:10.060
Or is it something that could
be a potentially harmful

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:11.340
application?

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:14.050
And so every Android device
that has the Google applications

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:16.210
on it does these
real-time checks

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:17.680
at the time of installation.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.410
And then, again, in the
background on an ongoing basis

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:22.690
we check all of the
applications on the device

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:24.648
to see whether or not it
could be a potentially

00:13:24.648 --> 00:13:25.906
harmful application.

00:13:25.906 --> 00:13:27.280
That has incredible
consequences,

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:29.320
which we'll talk about
it in just a second,

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:33.880
in terms of how clean we've
been able to keep the ecosystem.

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.550
Another thing that we
have on Android devices

00:13:36.550 --> 00:13:38.770
is what we call
the sensor network.

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.030
Sensor network is exactly
what you'd expect.

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:44.200
It's a network of
sensors on these devices

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.500
that checks for non
application-based security

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:48.370
risks.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:51.670
So beyond verifying the
apps, we also are looking at

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.380
is this device
connecting to a network

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:54.730
that's potentially hostile?

00:13:54.730 --> 00:13:58.270
Are we seeing attempted
exploitation of media file

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:00.110
formats on these devices?

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.693
And I'll talk a little bit about
what the consequences of that

00:14:02.693 --> 00:14:03.760
is.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:06.790
So we've got that across
a large number of devices.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.597
Developer APIs, I
think, are probably

00:14:08.597 --> 00:14:10.930
one of the things that are
even more interesting for you

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:14.200
folks, which is taking the
information from Verify

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:18.130
Apps and the sensor network and
exposing that so that you can

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:20.417
incorporate the intelligence
that Google is generating

00:14:20.417 --> 00:14:22.000
about what's going
on in the ecosystem

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.920
into your own security
model, and into how

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.980
you're thinking about those
devices and those applications.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:28.909
So you can basically
say, I don't

00:14:28.909 --> 00:14:30.700
want to have my app at
work if there's ever

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:32.380
been a piece of
malware on this device.

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:34.463
And Google's going to
provide the services for you

00:14:34.463 --> 00:14:35.520
to be able to do that.

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:38.290
And the last service that we
have there is finding your lost

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:39.760
device, right?

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.381
Or being able to respond in
the event that a device is lost

00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:42.880
or stolen.

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.770
The net of these services
is incredible visibility

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:50.580
into the ecosystem.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.710
And to give you a
flavor for it, we've

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.620
got over a billion and a half
devices that are protected

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.140
right now with these services.

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:00.600
They're checking in on
a very regular basis.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.330
So on average it's about
once every other day,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:07.080
where we're doing about 750
million scans of the ecosystem,

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:10.944
individual devices around the
ecosystem on a daily basis.

00:15:10.944 --> 00:15:13.110
And the number of applications
that we're looking at

00:15:13.110 --> 00:15:14.580
is also incredibly large.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:16.627
Because that's one of
the largest concerns that

00:15:16.627 --> 00:15:18.960
exist in the ecosystem is
what are the applications that

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.970
are there.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.540
The result of that is
that we're able to manage

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:28.020
the level of risk in the
ecosystem quite effectively.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:31.020
This is a graph
going back to 2014--

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:33.450
so almost three
years at this point--

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.540
showing the rate of
devices having what

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:39.150
we classify as a potentially
harmful application

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.410
installed on a device.

00:15:41.410 --> 00:15:46.050
This is a malware rate,
to put it quite bluntly,

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:49.380
for the world.

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.710
And I want to call that out--
most malware rates that exist

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:55.230
are from highly
managed environments.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.150
If you're in an
enterprise, you might

00:15:57.150 --> 00:15:59.870
be looking at-- what's
the frequency with which

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:02.850
a piece of malware
gets onto my Mac fleet,

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:10.780
or gets onto my iPhone fleet,
or gets onto my Windows fleet?

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:13.440
For the desktop platforms,
those numbers often

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.600
are on the order of 10% or so.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:20.255
A managed environment
with users who are--

00:16:20.255 --> 00:16:23.880
have active anti-virus
running on their device,

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:27.230
on their desktop platform, they
might even have white listing.

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:29.160
There may be other
characteristics or security

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.080
defenses that are in
place on those devices.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:33.480
But they still
have a malware rate

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:35.160
that's roughly an
order of magnitude

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:39.000
higher than what we've seen
on unmanaged Android devices

00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:41.190
worldwide.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.620
So a very different statistic.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.826
And we see sort of roughly an
order of magnitude cleaner.

00:16:47.826 --> 00:16:49.200
In fact, if you
look at something

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.960
that's a little bit more
similar to what your enterprise

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.780
fleet might look like, it would
be the second number, which

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:58.290
is for Android devices that are
only installing applications

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:01.930
from Google Play, what do we
see as their malware rate?

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:06.510
And here it's actually
about 120th of the rate

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.609
that you see for the average
across the ecosystem.

00:17:08.609 --> 00:17:09.900
It's 0.05.

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.319
So a full order of
magnitude cleaner.

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:15.270
And that's like the basic,
most simple management

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:17.246
that you can do is
to just say only

00:17:17.246 --> 00:17:19.079
allow installation of
apps from Google Play.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:20.454
If you take
additional steps that

00:17:20.454 --> 00:17:23.010
are provided through EMMs,
like white listing or filtering

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.468
the applications, you could
imagine that you could cut this

00:17:25.468 --> 00:17:27.579
down even more substantially.

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:29.310
So we're in an
incredibly clean place

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:33.060
for the overall ecosystem, which
is great if you're doing Beta C

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:36.092
or delivering applications
directly to consumers.

00:17:36.092 --> 00:17:38.550
And if you're talking about
managing within your own fleet,

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:40.883
you're able to get much, much
cleaner than that as well.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.600
These scans also
give us visibility

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:50.117
into exploitation of issues
that are found out in the wild.

00:17:50.117 --> 00:17:52.200
We have a sensor network
that's been delivered out

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:55.110
to over 1.4 billion devices.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:58.540
And we're able to look
back both in real time

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:02.160
but also historically to
understand the level of risk

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:04.950
that devices might
be exposed to.

00:18:04.950 --> 00:18:07.170
This is a chart.

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:09.930
These are the three
vulnerabilities

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:13.290
that have received probably
the most attention on Android

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:15.960
over the last four years.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:19.110
They were sort of the
marquee Android vulnerability

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:25.110
at the 2013, '14, and
'15 blackout conferences

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:29.190
held in Las Vegas in sort
of the middle of the summer.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:33.940
So master key, fake ID, and
Stagefright sort of in order.

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:37.200
Two of these are vulnerabilities
that specifically

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:40.024
affected the verification
of signatures

00:18:40.024 --> 00:18:41.190
at the time of installation.

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.860
And so we have
perfect visibility

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:47.010
for devices that have Verify
Apps and Safetynet turned on

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.294
into the level of exploitation,
because we actually

00:18:50.294 --> 00:18:52.710
checked every single install
that happens on those devices

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:53.543
through Verify Apps.

00:18:53.543 --> 00:18:56.820
So we can go back and
we can with confidence

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:00.180
say there was zero exploitation
before those issues became

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:01.240
public.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:03.152
And that even after
they became public,

00:19:03.152 --> 00:19:04.860
the level of exploitation
that took place

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.480
was extraordinarily low.

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:10.260
And then you can contrast that
to the level of communication

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.970
that existed in the media.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.250
Often what I like to do is
look at our data and say,

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:15.840
did more people
read about how scary

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:17.910
this thing was relative
to the number of people

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:18.660
who were affected?

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:20.702
And almost uniformly that's
going to be the case.

00:19:20.702 --> 00:19:23.326
There are a lot more people that
are frightened by these things

00:19:23.326 --> 00:19:24.900
than are actually
affected by them,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:26.950
which puts us in a
pretty good place.

00:19:26.950 --> 00:19:29.490
Stagefright was a watershed,
I think, for the Android

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:31.140
ecosystem at large.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.710
In terms of the
effect that it had,

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:35.220
it ended up driving
much more rapid adoption

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:37.690
of security bulletins
and security patches

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:38.460
in the ecosystem.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:40.500
We'll talk more about
that in just a second.

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:42.210
But what's interesting
about it is here

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.880
we are going on
a year and a half

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:46.800
after that issue became public.

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:50.386
And there still are no confirmed
instances of any exploitation.

00:19:50.386 --> 00:19:52.260
One of the things that
was really fascinating

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.810
over the last couple of
days is taking a look

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.690
at a new trove of
security vulnerabilities

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.550
and realizing that
none of them affected

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:02.760
media server, none of them
referred to Stagefright.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:06.570
So despite the
level of attention

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.682
that was focused on
that particular issue,

00:20:08.682 --> 00:20:10.890
it doesn't seem to have had
an actual effect in terms

00:20:10.890 --> 00:20:13.000
of the user security
that's out there.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.480
Again, not claiming
that it didn't.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:19.000
I'm claiming that we haven't
seen it, which is, I think,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:21.240
pretty interesting that
the distance between what's

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:22.906
happening in the
ecosystem and the level

00:20:22.906 --> 00:20:27.410
of communication around risk
that exists in the ecosystem.

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:29.320
So if you're interested
in the services

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:33.190
and getting a deeper dive
into how those services work,

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:34.960
Edward Cunningham,
who is the product

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:36.880
manager for those services,
is going to talk tomorrow.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:38.754
He's going to go into
how we're using machine

00:20:38.754 --> 00:20:41.560
learning to detect specific
types of attacks that might be

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:43.441
taking place against
users, some of the work

00:20:43.441 --> 00:20:45.190
that we've been doing
with neural networks

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:49.407
and detecting those, and talk
through a number of areas

00:20:49.407 --> 00:20:50.740
of investment that we're making.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:53.323
So I think that is actually one
of the most interesting things

00:20:53.323 --> 00:20:57.764
that's going on is those
advancements and services.

00:20:57.764 --> 00:20:59.680
The other area that I
want to talk a bit about

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:02.850
is ecosystem updates.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:07.180
And there's two different
areas that we're investing in.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:09.171
Google, obviously,
has an obligation

00:21:09.171 --> 00:21:11.170
to build the most secure
version of the platform

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:13.990
that we possibly can and to
get that out to as many people

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:15.130
as we possibly can.

00:21:15.130 --> 00:21:16.690
But there are two
other parties that

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:19.870
have an impact on the
security of a device.

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.490
The first is
application developers,

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:25.269
and let me talk a little
bit about my experience

00:21:25.269 --> 00:21:27.310
as someone who came from
the world of application

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:28.510
developers.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:32.950
Prior to being at
Google, I was working

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:38.140
on security for desktop
applications, head of security

00:21:38.140 --> 00:21:39.580
at Adobe.

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.580
And what was interesting to
me in the Windows ecosystem

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:45.940
is for the first
handful of years

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:49.930
all of the focus on security was
at the operating system level.

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:55.150
And then circa 2004, 2005
there was a transition where

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:58.540
exploitation shifted
from the platform itself

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.580
into the key applications.

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:02.320
And what were those
key applications?

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:08.259
Adobe PDF and Reader, Adobe
Flash were two of them.

00:22:08.259 --> 00:22:09.550
The browsers were another one--

00:22:09.550 --> 00:22:11.975
Firefox, IE.

00:22:11.975 --> 00:22:15.066
And Chrome wasn't
there at the time but

00:22:15.066 --> 00:22:17.440
quickly became one of the
areas that was the focal point,

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.450
and it is still today.

00:22:19.450 --> 00:22:22.000
We also began to see a lot of
exploitation that was targeted

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:24.110
at other document formats.

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:26.200
And so what was
interesting was no matter

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.710
how hard we had made the
platform looking at Windows,

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:32.800
there was this shift in
where the weak spot was.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:34.690
And so on the Android
side, we wanted

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.090
to make sure that that
shift doesn't take place.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:38.920
And so we're very
proactively beginning

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:41.200
to look at
application developers

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:43.690
and make sure that they're
in a much better place.

00:22:43.690 --> 00:22:45.970
By default, since
most applications

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:47.890
on Android are written in Java--

00:22:47.890 --> 00:22:50.740
which is a typesafe language
and a memory safe language--

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:53.810
by default, they're
already in a better place.

00:22:53.810 --> 00:22:55.960
But we wanted to see if we
could do more than that.

00:22:55.960 --> 00:22:58.840
So the approach that
we took was beginning

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:03.460
to use the scanning services
that we had built to find

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:07.450
malware and repurposing that.

00:23:07.450 --> 00:23:09.100
And so the repurposing
that we did

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:12.700
was to look for
basic vulnerabilities

00:23:12.700 --> 00:23:15.670
in applications.

00:23:15.670 --> 00:23:19.140
This is a picture of the
set of new technologies

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:21.640
that we introduced where we
were looking for vulnerabilities

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:23.170
over the last year.

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:29.800
They range from things like
libupnp and Apache Cordova

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:31.960
where what we were
looking for was

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:35.410
an old version of a library
that had a known vulnerability.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:38.032
We scan all the applications
that are in Google Play,

00:23:38.032 --> 00:23:39.740
and then we notify
those developers, hey,

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:41.680
you're running an
old version of this.

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:44.820
In many instances, the
developer's like, hey, thanks.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:45.820
I didn't even know that.

00:23:45.820 --> 00:23:47.530
And they immediately fix it.

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:50.219
In some instances, the
developer's like, clueless,

00:23:50.219 --> 00:23:52.510
hasn't been doing anything,
isn't paying any attention,

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:54.520
and the app continues to exist.

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:58.150
The next time that they go
to update their application,

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:02.360
add a new feature, ship a
new release, get new users,

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:06.730
they're told you can't do that
until that application has

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:09.880
had the vulnerability
that we identified fixed.

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:12.820
And so this has ended up
having a very significant sort

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:17.140
of-- what I think of as raising
the base level of security that

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:19.670
exists in the applications.

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:22.150
So the simplest one is
something like checking

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:25.450
for a vulnerable
version of a library.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:27.610
There are more
sophisticated ones as well,

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:31.210
like the SSL Error Handler,
where what we were looking for

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:35.950
is whether an application
is handling SSL

00:24:35.950 --> 00:24:38.260
errors in a way that's safe.

00:24:38.260 --> 00:24:40.010
If you check to
see-- you've enabled

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:42.250
SSL on a particular
network connection,

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:43.780
you get back an SSL error.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.217
If what you say is,
that's OK, just connect,

00:24:46.217 --> 00:24:47.800
that's probably not
what was intended,

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:49.850
or you wouldn't have
turned on SSL at all.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:51.250
But it's also a
very common thing

00:24:51.250 --> 00:24:52.870
that a developer will
do in a testing phase

00:24:52.870 --> 00:24:53.953
for an application, right?

00:24:53.953 --> 00:24:57.222
They'll say, I don't really
have a current certificate

00:24:57.222 --> 00:24:59.680
on my test server, and so I'm
going to allow the connection

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:00.960
to go through.

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:02.980
And so what we've
done here is we've

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:04.510
looked for those
types of instances

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.135
where the default
handler is one that's

00:25:06.135 --> 00:25:08.050
allowing all connections.

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.730
We flag the application.

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:12.060
We tell the developer,
look, this is the problem.

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:14.500
And then they have the option
of either disabling SSL,

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:16.180
if that's what was
their intention,

00:25:16.180 --> 00:25:18.985
or fixing it so that the SSL
Error Handler is actually

00:25:18.985 --> 00:25:20.904
a sane SSL Error Handler.

00:25:20.904 --> 00:25:22.570
So there's a variety
of different levels

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.370
of sophistication on
the types of warnings

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:25.953
that we're able to
give to developers.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:30.990
I mentioned that
in some instances

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:34.770
we're using sort of more
strong enforcement mechanisms

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:37.630
through Google Play to try to
make sure that these devices

00:25:37.630 --> 00:25:41.080
or that these applications
are being brought up to date.

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:43.260
And so that's had a
pretty significant impact.

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:45.990
This is a graph of the
number of applications

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:49.140
that have been fixed as a
result of us delivering warnings

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:49.750
to them.

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:54.570
So in 2016, almost 300,000
unique applications

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.130
that had their
security improved.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.650
So incredible scale
in terms of being

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:01.341
able to deliver security
protections at the application

00:26:01.341 --> 00:26:01.840
level.

00:26:04.610 --> 00:26:08.720
So application developers
are one area of focus for us.

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:12.980
Another is around devices and
the devices that are being

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:15.770
built using Android software.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:17.270
As you may know--

00:26:17.270 --> 00:26:20.600
I expect many people do, but
there probably are a few that

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:22.070
don't--

00:26:22.070 --> 00:26:24.120
Google doesn't ship
software on devices,

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.370
with the exception of the
Pixel and the Nexus devices.

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:28.790
So roughly 1% of the
Android ecosystem

00:26:28.790 --> 00:26:31.205
is binaries that was--

00:26:31.205 --> 00:26:33.410
it consisted of devices
that have binaries

00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:34.670
that were built by Google.

00:26:34.670 --> 00:26:38.930
The other 99%, an OEM takes
source code from an open source

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:42.680
project, compiles that,
and then delivers that

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.360
as the software on
top of those devices.

00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:47.240
They go through certification,
and CDD, and a variety

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:48.982
of other compatibility
requirements

00:26:48.982 --> 00:26:51.440
in order to be able to make
sure that the devices work well

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:53.065
with the rest of the
Android ecosystem.

00:26:53.065 --> 00:26:55.520
But ultimately it's
the device maker

00:26:55.520 --> 00:26:57.490
that is responsible for
delivering the software

00:26:57.490 --> 00:26:58.290
onto those devices.

00:26:58.290 --> 00:26:59.706
And so one of the
great challenges

00:26:59.706 --> 00:27:02.240
is how do we make sure that
the devices that are out there

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:04.520
have the most recent
version of the software,

00:27:04.520 --> 00:27:08.210
and that any changes that
might have been made by the OEM

00:27:08.210 --> 00:27:11.120
don't negatively affect
security on those devices?

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:14.105
And so we work really closely
with device makers to make sure

00:27:14.105 --> 00:27:16.550
that they're doing that.

00:27:16.550 --> 00:27:21.830
One of the other things that
we work with are carriers.

00:27:21.830 --> 00:27:24.080
So this is a picture to
give you a rough sense

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:27.500
for how many carriers there are
worldwide that we're working

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.450
with on an active basis.

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:31.670
So there are probably
a few more than this.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:33.780
This is a snapshot
taken not too long ago.

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.780
But there are 351
different carriers that

00:27:35.780 --> 00:27:37.460
are out there in the world.

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:39.920
Carriers-- in the
United States we're

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:44.960
talking about AT&amp;T, Verizon,
T-Mobile, Sprint, and a number

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:47.409
of other ones as well.

00:27:47.409 --> 00:27:48.950
Carriers play a very
significant role

00:27:48.950 --> 00:27:51.860
because often they
are the source

00:27:51.860 --> 00:27:53.300
of purchase for a device.

00:27:53.300 --> 00:27:55.490
You go into a carrier
store, you buy a device.

00:27:55.490 --> 00:27:58.902
They also have requirements
about the functionality

00:27:58.902 --> 00:28:00.110
that exists on those devices.

00:28:00.110 --> 00:28:04.370
They're very strongly involved
in the delivery of software

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:05.230
on those devices.

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:07.490
They require applications
that might be installed.

00:28:07.490 --> 00:28:10.100
They require configuration
to work on their network.

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:12.860
In some instances, the network
protocols are different.

00:28:12.860 --> 00:28:15.320
CDMA versus GSM-- although
that's tending to break down

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:16.340
at this point--

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:17.965
it was one of the
variations that would

00:28:17.965 --> 00:28:19.874
exist within the US networks.

00:28:19.874 --> 00:28:21.540
And so we're working
with these carriers

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:23.831
to define what software is
going to be on these devices

00:28:23.831 --> 00:28:25.430
and what the
requirements are for it.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:29.660
Now, historically,
cell phones did not

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.480
get updated basically at all.

00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:35.510
And there was a very
strong resistance

00:28:35.510 --> 00:28:38.146
to updating software on those
devices because of stability

00:28:38.146 --> 00:28:39.020
at the network level.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:42.440
And so the carriers,
for many years,

00:28:42.440 --> 00:28:45.050
had been defining
a testing regime

00:28:45.050 --> 00:28:49.670
around software for these
devices that was designed--

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:50.947
correctly, I would argue--

00:28:50.947 --> 00:28:53.030
to minimize the rate at
which device updates could

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:54.890
be delivered to those devices.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:58.010
They didn't want devices to be
changing because change created

00:28:58.010 --> 00:29:00.600
instability both
at a network level,

00:29:00.600 --> 00:29:03.140
but also at an
individual user level.

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:04.970
If the software
changed, that was bad.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:07.520
There are other factors
as well around congestion

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:09.330
and consumption of bandwidth.

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:13.870
And so in the
Android environment

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:16.444
we're dealing with a software
stack that's much more complex.

00:29:16.444 --> 00:29:17.860
We're dealing with
vulnerabilities

00:29:17.860 --> 00:29:18.734
that are being found.

00:29:18.734 --> 00:29:20.620
We talked about
Stagefright and Master Key

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:22.200
and a few of the
other ones earlier.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:24.430
And so there was a bit
of a cognitive dissonance

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:26.119
between the approach
the carriers

00:29:26.119 --> 00:29:28.660
had adopted of minimizing the
rate at which the updates could

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:31.160
be delivered, maximizing the
amount of testing that could be

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:32.743
done, and the need
to deliver security

00:29:32.743 --> 00:29:34.040
updates in a timely fashion.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:37.086
So over the last year or so--

00:29:37.086 --> 00:29:38.460
but going back
further than that.

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:39.770
But for sure over
the last year or so,

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:41.520
we've been working a
lot with the carriers

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:44.380
to differentiate between
updates to devices

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:46.870
that were nonsecurity
specific versus ones

00:29:46.870 --> 00:29:48.670
that were security specific.

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:54.370
And so beginning of 2016
roughly, the average update

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:58.210
to an Android
device, at a minimum,

00:29:58.210 --> 00:29:59.660
from the point
where an OEM said,

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.785
I'm done with this update,
I'm ready to ship it out

00:30:01.785 --> 00:30:02.620
to customers--

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:05.620
until it had gone through
carrier testing and approval

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:08.162
was on the order of
six to nine weeks.

00:30:08.162 --> 00:30:10.120
What was also interesting
about that is usually

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.310
that had to be scheduled
another six to nine weeks ahead

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:12.590
of time.

00:30:12.590 --> 00:30:14.740
So we're really talking
about an 18-week period

00:30:14.740 --> 00:30:17.590
basically from when
the OEM is done

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:20.680
to when an update could make it
into the hands of a consumer.

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:23.950
So very difficult to
deliver security updates

00:30:23.950 --> 00:30:25.450
in a timely fashion.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:27.040
Over the last year
and a half, we've

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:28.750
worked with the
carriers-- basically

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:30.550
with all the major
carriers, about 40 or 50

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:33.190
of them worldwide-- to make
sure that if an update was

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.340
a security only update,
then that could be delivered

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:38.030
in less than a week.

00:30:38.030 --> 00:30:40.600
So they've gone
through and said, OK,

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:43.090
if issues are in
security bulletins,

00:30:43.090 --> 00:30:45.341
and if we're provided with
the software from Google,

00:30:45.341 --> 00:30:47.590
then we're going to be willing
to let those go through

00:30:47.590 --> 00:30:49.006
at a much faster
approval process.

00:30:49.006 --> 00:30:50.599
And so we've taken
advantage of that

00:30:50.599 --> 00:30:53.140
to make sure that we're able to
deliver security updates very

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:55.210
quickly to devices.

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:59.660
So we've sort of eliminated some
of the cost associated with it,

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:01.450
but we haven't
eliminated a lot of it.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:03.366
So the next thing that
we've been investing in

00:31:03.366 --> 00:31:07.570
over the last year or so is
in working with specific OEMs

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:10.877
to try to refine what their
development process looks like.

00:31:10.877 --> 00:31:12.460
And so I want to
give you a little bit

00:31:12.460 --> 00:31:17.410
of a flavor for where we've
been focusing on our effort.

00:31:17.410 --> 00:31:21.700
So beginning of last
year, 2016, we sat down

00:31:21.700 --> 00:31:25.060
and we said, OK, we're trying
to change an ecosystem that

00:31:25.060 --> 00:31:29.212
has over 60,000 unique devices
in it to get it to a point

00:31:29.212 --> 00:31:30.670
where it's able to
deliver security

00:31:30.670 --> 00:31:32.014
updates in a timely fashion.

00:31:32.014 --> 00:31:33.430
How are we going
to approach that?

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:36.619
So we chopped off the top
OEMs in the flagship devices

00:31:36.619 --> 00:31:37.410
for those top OEMs.

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.490
And we said, we're
going to focus initially

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:43.960
on updating just those devices,
the ones that you might

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:47.110
be familiar with because you've
seen them like, on a billboard,

00:31:47.110 --> 00:31:49.214
or they cost $600.

00:31:49.214 --> 00:31:50.380
And they're receiving a lot.

00:31:50.380 --> 00:31:52.210
Those are the ones
that we think is

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.550
the best opportunity to
work with OEMs to refactor

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:55.460
their approach.

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:57.804
So we talked to
the top five OEMs.

00:31:57.804 --> 00:32:00.220
We said, let's look at just
your flagship devices because,

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:02.178
remember, a lot of these
OEMs produce literally

00:32:02.178 --> 00:32:03.920
dozens if not hundreds.

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.000
So it ended up being about 29
device models across those five

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:07.792
OEMs.

00:32:07.792 --> 00:32:09.250
The flagship in
North America might

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:11.430
be different from the
flagship in Europe,

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:13.540
might be different from
the flagship in Asia.

00:32:13.540 --> 00:32:15.331
So there's a little
bit of complexity there

00:32:15.331 --> 00:32:18.460
that may not be
obvious as a consumer.

00:32:18.460 --> 00:32:20.904
I like to anchor this
and say, at this point

00:32:20.904 --> 00:32:22.570
you're already talking
about more device

00:32:22.570 --> 00:32:25.150
models than any other platform.

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:27.810
You've already-- like, we're not
talking about the full range.

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:30.100
We're not even talking about a
tiny fraction of the ecosystem.

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:32.099
But just looking at these
you're already talking

00:32:32.099 --> 00:32:34.450
about more complexity
than any other ecosystem

00:32:34.450 --> 00:32:37.480
that exists in the world.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:39.520
Now, I mentioned
carriers, and the fact

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:43.240
that carriers like to have
customization for devices.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:46.382
And so those 29 different device
models when you sort of blow

00:32:46.382 --> 00:32:48.090
them up across all
the different carriers

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:51.010
and the customisations,
what you end up with is--

00:32:51.010 --> 00:32:56.050
just looking at the flagship
models for the top five OEMs--

00:32:56.050 --> 00:33:01.750
5,000 unique builds
of Android that

00:33:01.750 --> 00:33:04.690
are currently being serviced
that are flagship devices.

00:33:04.690 --> 00:33:05.607
OK.

00:33:05.607 --> 00:33:07.690
That's 5,000 different
development teams out there

00:33:07.690 --> 00:33:08.800
in the world.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:11.380
Think about that-- it's
5,000 Git repositories.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:13.560
It's 5,000 QA teams.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:16.880
It's, I don't know, probably
10,000 different VPEs

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.379
of engineering, all of
whom need to be working

00:33:19.379 --> 00:33:21.670
in synchronicity in order to
be able to deliver updates

00:33:21.670 --> 00:33:23.280
on these devices.

00:33:23.280 --> 00:33:26.200
And so what started
as a really simple,

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:29.380
hey, let's just update
devices on a monthly basis,

00:33:29.380 --> 00:33:31.920
turned out to be a little bit
more complicated than that.

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:35.261
And so we've been working very
steadily with those top five

00:33:35.261 --> 00:33:37.010
OEMs and others in the
ecosystem as well--

00:33:37.010 --> 00:33:40.240
but for the purposes of this
we'll focus on those top five--

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:42.160
to make sure that
these 5,000 devices

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:44.530
are being brought up to date.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:47.620
The sort of output, because
who cares about the process?

00:33:47.620 --> 00:33:49.790
What you really care
about is the output.

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:53.650
The output is, the beginning
of 2016, about somewhere

00:33:53.650 --> 00:33:56.050
on the order of, I
say, less than 20%.

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:57.880
I think it's on the
order of 12% to 13%

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:03.350
of devices were up to
date in the last 60 days.

00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:05.950
So basically, the last security
bulletin that was available

00:34:05.950 --> 00:34:07.750
was on those devices.

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:09.159
That's just for flagships.

00:34:09.159 --> 00:34:13.300
The number was even lower
for the broader ecosystem.

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:15.820
By the end of 2016, we
had brought that number up

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:17.800
to over 50%.

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:21.880
So on the flagship devices there
was a very significant change.

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:25.750
Over 50% of devices
that are up to date.

00:34:25.750 --> 00:34:27.114
That's not-- we're not done.

00:34:27.114 --> 00:34:28.989
I just want to make sure
I'm expressing that.

00:34:28.989 --> 00:34:30.639
Like, this is great progress.

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:31.840
20 to 50-- huge, right?

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:33.820
The line is going
up to the right.

00:34:33.820 --> 00:34:36.929
But, you know, there's
another 45-ish percent

00:34:36.929 --> 00:34:39.429
that we need to figure out how
to get those patched as well.

00:34:39.429 --> 00:34:42.257
And then again, this is
just for flagship devices.

00:34:42.257 --> 00:34:43.840
If you're looking
in your environment,

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:47.170
you can hone in a little bit
on specific characteristics

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:51.380
that may make your
environment safer.

00:34:51.380 --> 00:34:53.989
The flagship devices
that were brought

00:34:53.989 --> 00:35:00.500
in through top carriers in
North America or in Europe

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:03.780
have an even higher
patch rate, over 70%.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:06.425
This does mean, by the way, that
if you bought your device not

00:35:06.425 --> 00:35:08.550
from one of those carriers,
then you're necessarily

00:35:08.550 --> 00:35:10.591
going to have a percentage
that must be below 50%

00:35:10.591 --> 00:35:12.030
because they average out, right?

00:35:12.030 --> 00:35:12.870
So you want to
think a little bit

00:35:12.870 --> 00:35:15.161
about where you're sourcing
devices from and using them

00:35:15.161 --> 00:35:16.045
in your environment.

00:35:19.850 --> 00:35:22.720
You also have the ability to
be more selective about which

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:26.110
devices you want to purchase
if you're in an environment

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:28.510
where you're thinking
about how to source them.

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:32.110
One device that we recommend
from Google is the Pixel.

00:35:32.110 --> 00:35:35.200
We consistently have an
update rate of over 90%.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:37.630
Patches are made
immediately available.

00:35:37.630 --> 00:35:40.507
I can get you details about
other manufacturers that

00:35:40.507 --> 00:35:41.590
also provide that as well.

00:35:41.590 --> 00:35:42.550
They're a little
bit more sensitive

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.800
about us talking about the
devices that they're making

00:35:44.800 --> 00:35:45.910
and their update rates.

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:48.190
But we can get you more
information about that.

00:35:48.190 --> 00:35:50.523
If you have questions, just
feel free to reach out to me

00:35:50.523 --> 00:35:52.690
and I can help get you in
contact with somebody that

00:35:52.690 --> 00:35:55.620
can get you that information.

00:35:55.620 --> 00:35:57.790
There's a really
simple way if you

00:35:57.790 --> 00:35:59.480
want to have an
update rate that's

00:35:59.480 --> 00:36:03.160
a really simple, good one.

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:06.220
We provide an API, which is
build version security patch

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:07.330
level.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.600
If you're an EMM, you can
write a policy that says,

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:14.410
I only want to be running on
devices that are up to date.

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:17.380
You can say 90 days, 60 days.

00:36:17.380 --> 00:36:21.790
I would recommend 90
days simply because there

00:36:21.790 --> 00:36:24.365
are a number of devices that--

00:36:24.365 --> 00:36:26.740
basically because your update
cycle is on a 30-day cycle,

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:28.030
roughly.

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:31.510
And so if devices are more than
a couple of weeks out of date,

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:32.830
they'll very quickly--

00:36:32.830 --> 00:36:37.170
A, they'll almost every month
end up around 30 days, and then

00:36:37.170 --> 00:36:41.500
B, it's not uncommon that
they'll end up going past 60.

00:36:41.500 --> 00:36:45.002
And so giving your
users the opportunity

00:36:45.002 --> 00:36:46.960
to actually apply the
patch, get it up to date,

00:36:46.960 --> 00:36:49.940
is the approach that
we would recommend.

00:36:49.940 --> 00:36:54.800
So we generally say 90 days is
probably the right approach.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:57.220
And so that gives you a
sense for the progress

00:36:57.220 --> 00:36:58.120
that we've made.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:36:59.536
Before we close
on that, I do want

00:36:59.536 --> 00:37:01.660
to say, I think over
the next two years

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:05.980
or so we'll continue to see
an increase in broad based

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:08.946
adoption of security updates
around the Android ecosystem.

00:37:08.946 --> 00:37:10.570
That's my sort of
forecast is that it's

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:13.070
going take about two to three
years before we get to a point

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:16.940
where you have 80% of all the
devices that are up to date.

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:19.510
That it's taking
time for the OEMs

00:37:19.510 --> 00:37:22.600
to ingest and modify
their processes

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:25.210
to be able to deliver updates
on a more regular basis.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:31.910
But all the trajectory is
in the right direction.

00:37:31.910 --> 00:37:34.040
The last thing that
I want to mention

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:38.450
is if data is your thing and you
want to know a little bit more

00:37:38.450 --> 00:37:41.150
about Android
security and the work

00:37:41.150 --> 00:37:44.630
that we've been doing there,
every year for the last three

00:37:44.630 --> 00:37:48.140
years we've produced an annual
security year in review.

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:50.810
It's about an 80-page
document this year.

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:52.812
It'll be coming out in
the next couple of weeks.

00:37:52.812 --> 00:37:54.770
And I would encourage
you to take a look at it.

00:37:54.770 --> 00:37:56.900
It breaks down all
the malware data.

00:37:56.900 --> 00:37:58.779
It breaks down update
rates, breaks down

00:37:58.779 --> 00:38:00.320
a lot of the features
and investments

00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:02.150
that we've been
making in that space

00:38:02.150 --> 00:38:04.040
so you can really
understand what's going on

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:07.820
across the ecosystem at large.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:09.790
And the last thing that
I want to sort of note

00:38:09.790 --> 00:38:13.810
is I think often in the
security space we focus

00:38:13.810 --> 00:38:17.500
so much on the technology that's
sort of at the platform level

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:21.070
or at the operating system level
that we lose sight of the fact

00:38:21.070 --> 00:38:23.560
that, especially in
the mobile space,

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:27.880
we've really already begun to
unlock business models that

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.250
were not possible previously.

00:38:30.250 --> 00:38:33.070
And we've done that from a
security standpoint, as well.

00:38:33.070 --> 00:38:35.080
This one cracks me
up a little bit.

00:38:35.080 --> 00:38:40.222
This is a picture of people
playing Pokemon Go in Japan.

00:38:40.222 --> 00:38:42.430
There are similar pictures
from around San Francisco.

00:38:42.430 --> 00:38:44.830
I just couldn't find one
that had quite as many heads

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:48.520
packed into a little space
all staring at their screens.

00:38:48.520 --> 00:38:52.810
And gaming in particular
on mobile devices

00:38:52.810 --> 00:38:56.501
has been an incredible
driver of security.

00:38:56.501 --> 00:38:58.000
I think the security
community often

00:38:58.000 --> 00:38:59.980
will sort of poo-poo it
as, ah, it's just DRM,

00:38:59.980 --> 00:39:01.750
or, ah, it's just games.

00:39:01.750 --> 00:39:04.390
But this is a billion
dollar business

00:39:04.390 --> 00:39:07.780
that depends upon the
hardest threat model, which

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:10.000
is that the person who has
the device in their hand

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:12.130
is trying to break it.

00:39:12.130 --> 00:39:15.010
And so the fact that they're
able to build this business

00:39:15.010 --> 00:39:17.950
depends on two things-- one,
it depends on the core security

00:39:17.950 --> 00:39:20.181
properties of the
platform, and then two,

00:39:20.181 --> 00:39:22.180
it depends upon the fact
that they as developers

00:39:22.180 --> 00:39:23.620
realized that they
needed to use some

00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:25.510
of the more sophisticated
features that were available

00:39:25.510 --> 00:39:26.380
and services.

00:39:26.380 --> 00:39:29.350
So they were one of the early
adopters of something called

00:39:29.350 --> 00:39:31.870
SafetyNet.Attestation
where Google tries

00:39:31.870 --> 00:39:34.270
to assess whether this is
a device that's currently

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:35.710
operating in a
good state or not,

00:39:35.710 --> 00:39:38.510
pass that information up to
the application developer.

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:42.054
So I think what's
interesting is they've

00:39:42.054 --> 00:39:44.470
managed to do that and build
that billion dollar business.

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:45.790
I don't have any expectation.

00:39:45.790 --> 00:39:49.300
Maybe some of you are building
your entire business on mobile.

00:39:49.300 --> 00:39:50.080
Maybe you're not.

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:51.760
But if you are, there
are other people

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:52.540
that are doing that as well.

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:54.331
And they're scaling to
billions of dollars,

00:39:54.331 --> 00:39:55.970
even in a very high
risk environment.

00:39:55.970 --> 00:39:57.970
And that's something that
I want to flag, right?

00:39:57.970 --> 00:40:00.460
Because I think, often,
especially in security

00:40:00.460 --> 00:40:05.850
there's this reversion
to change aversion that

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:08.550
makes it impossible for
people to say, oh, my gosh,

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:10.560
I couldn't do this on
Windows because there

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:13.490
was so much piracy, and there
was so much flexibility.

00:40:13.490 --> 00:40:17.040
But on mobile, it's actually not
just possible, it's happening.

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:20.130
And games are one of the
primary drivers of it.

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:22.560
Similarly, payment
infrastructure

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:24.570
is something that we've
seen very rapid adoption

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:27.270
of based on the security
properties of the platform.

00:40:27.270 --> 00:40:31.020
The fact that we're able to go
into major payment operators

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:34.565
and say, trust the platform,
here is the capabilities of it,

00:40:34.565 --> 00:40:35.440
is really incredible.

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:38.760
And it's something that these
folks depend upon trust zone,

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.220
they depend upon hardware
security in a way

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:43.000
that others haven't
been able to.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:44.970
And I think that's
one of the reasons why

00:40:44.970 --> 00:40:50.190
when you look at the most
targeted highest risk

00:40:50.190 --> 00:40:52.980
users in the world,
more and more we're

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:55.034
seeing an acknowledgement
that mobile devices are

00:40:55.034 --> 00:40:56.450
necessary for them
to communicate,

00:40:56.450 --> 00:40:58.494
and investments
that are being made

00:40:58.494 --> 00:41:00.910
are sufficient for them to be
able to use these platforms.

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:03.210
So it's a really
exciting time for me

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:07.380
to know that people who
are the most at risk,

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:10.710
whether that be world
leaders, or whether that

00:41:10.710 --> 00:41:13.740
be dissidents who
are being targeted,

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:15.240
they can rely on
the technology they

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:18.540
have in a way that never was
possible before from a security

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:19.040
standpoint.

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:22.930
So that's pretty
exciting for me.

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:25.650
So this was sort of
the think about how

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:27.660
it is relevant in your
environment as well

00:41:27.660 --> 00:41:30.200
and how to take advantage of it.

